  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14108577",
    "id": "q-161842",
    "status": "supported",
    "reason": null,
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-161842",
      "knowledge_point_id": "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing",
      "problem": "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ u-\\frac{1}{3}v\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ u-\\frac{1}{3}v\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2} & -v \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3{u}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})-(1)\\cdot (-v) \\\\  & =v-{u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{u}^{2} & <0 \\\\ v & <{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv={u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14108577/assets/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png)",
